Given the reasonable fabrication of well-constructed P@Pt@P-Au, the in vitro functional performances were next evaluated. To start with, considering the fact that the Zr6 clusters have strong affinity towards phosphate groups, the structure of PCN is relatively not stable in physiological conditions such as phosphate buffered solution (PBS).35 According to some reported literatures, negatively-charged FA with carboxyl groups were able to be easily introduced on the surface of PCN via coordination with the available binding sites of the $Z\mathrm{r}6$ clusters, leading to an improved stability of the PCN when dispersed in PBS.25 Moreover, PCN is positively charged, which potentially limits its further in vivo biomedical application. In this context, surface modification of FA is crucial in our work. As shown in the TEM images of Figure 2a, the stability of P@Pt@P-FA dispersed in PBS $(5~\mathrm{mM})$ was significantly improved compared with that of unmodified $\mathrm{P}\ @\mathrm{Pt}(\mathcal{O}\mathrm{P}$ . Besides, the successful modification with FA was further confirmed from the UV-vis-NIR absorbance spectra (Figure S5), the zeta potential change during the fabrication process (Figure 2b) and the improved stability of P@Pt@P-Au-FA in different physiological solutions (Figure S6).
Next, the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ catalytic ability of $\mathrm{P}\ @\mathrm{Pt}(\varpi\mathrm{P} $ or PCN dispersed in aqueous solution containing $300~\upmu\mathrm{M}~\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ was evaluated at $37~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Even after repetitively adding $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ for five times, the $\mathrm{P}\ @\mathrm{Pt}(\mathcal{Q}\mathrm{P}$ still maintained a superior catalytic activity and a quick complete depletion of the $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ within $10~\mathrm{{min}}$ (Figure 2c). In comparison, the PCN showed no response to $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ (Figure S7), demonstrating that the Pt NPs played a key role in effectively catalyzing the conversion of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ to $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . Then, with the excellent $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ -evolving ability, the PDT performances of the $\mathrm{P}\ @\mathrm{Pt}(\varpi\mathrm{P} $ under laser irradiation were examined by an ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ sensor of 9,10-Anthracenediyl-bis(methylene)dimalonic acid (ABDA), especially towards hypoxia-mimicking conditions with or without the addition of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ (Figure 2d; Figure S8). Apparently, the ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ yields for $\mathrm{P}\ @\mathrm{Pt}(\varpi\mathrm{P}$ and PCN were both fatally weakened under oxygen-deficient conditions, due to the inherent $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ -dependent property of PDT. Nevertheless, $\mathrm{P}\ @\mathrm{Pt}(\varpi\mathrm{P} $ showed a significantly enhanced production of ${}^{1}{\bf O}_{2}$ after the addition of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ compared with that of PCN. As a consequence, $\mathrm{P}\ @\mathrm{Pt}(\mathcal{O}\mathrm{P}$ indeed achieved hypoxia relief in the presence of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and in turn enhanced the PDT effect upon laser exposure in vitro.
Furthermore, in order to confirm the glucose oxidase-mimicking ability of the Au NPs in $\mathbf{P}@\mathbf{P}^{\mathrm{t}}@\mathbf{P}\ –\mathbf{Au}$ , gluconic acid as the product of oxidized glucose was analyzed according to the reported colorimetric assay by UV-vis-NIR absorbance spectroscopy.34 As shown in Figure 2e, time-dependent production of gluconic acid catalyzed by P@Pt@P-Au was confirmed as the appearance of strong wide absorbance band at $450{\mathrm{-}}700\ \mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S9). Besides, the $\mathrm{P}\ @\mathrm{Pt}(\mathcal{Q}\mathrm{P}\cdot$ - Au maintained the excellent and comparable catalase-mimicking ability towards such a high concentration of $30\mathrm{mM}\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ as well as the PDT efficiency with that of $\mathrm{P}\ @\mathrm{Pt}(\varpi\mathrm{P} $ (Figure S10; Figure S11). Taking together, these results confirmed that the Pt and Au NPs in our system can act as effective catalase mimics and glucose oxidase mimics in vitro, respectively.
